{
  "dom_id": "step-q351589",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227267/assets/f78efc78b9f7cded50b29ba63855d81ff4e9ee8b7d12aa9673fac1cb7644e50a.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+1}{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+1}-{e}^{a+1}) \\\\  & =5(e-0) \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x=5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5e+5e \\\\  & =10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
